{"abstract":"Designing new technologies to support synchronous interaction across distances has for many years focused on creating symmetry for participation between geographically distributed actors. Symmetry in synchronous interaction has, to some extent, been achieved technologically (while multiple social, historical, political, and hierarchical concerns continue to exist) and proven empirically in the increased use of remote-work technologies that were used during the pandemic. However, synchronous interaction in hybrid work is achieved differently, since the asymmetry produced by some participants being collocated while others geographically distributed introduces increased complexities for such interactions. Focusing on this challenge, we ask: To what extent can symmetry in cooperative work engagements be achieved in hybrid work contexts? We explore this question by interrogating multiple different empirical examples of synchronous hybrid interaction collected across different organizations, activities, and events. We found that the effort required to accomplish hybrid work includes additional articulation work necessary for bounding multiple intertwined artefacts across sites, devices, and applications. Further, the multiple artefacts setup across sites, combined with asymmetric collocation across participants, produce incongruence in technological frames of reference for each participant. All participants in hybrid work have only partial access to the hybrid setup, and no single person has access to the complete setup. The incongruence in technological frames produces insurmountable gaps in collaboration, causing all hybrid work situations to be characterized fundamentally by asymmetric relationships. We argue that symmetry in hybrid synchronous interaction is impossible to attain in attempts to solve this problem through design. Instead, we propose that designers of cooperative technologies for hybrid work shift towards developing artefact-ecologies supporting hybrid work, focusing on asymmetry as a necessary feature. Fundamentally, the design strategy should explore novel ways of taking advantage of the multiple different artefact-ecologies which serve as the foundation for the hybrid collaboration. Instead of striving for symmetry, we propose to feature asymmetric conditions in future technology designs for hybrid interaction.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Bjørn, Pernille","Busboom, Juliane","Duckert, Melanie","Bødker, Susanne","Shklovski, Irina","Hoggan, Eve","Dunn, Kellie","Mu, Qianqian","Barkhuus, Louise","Boulus-Rodje, Nina"],"cuid":["S03-097.66-767390"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Bjørn, Pernille"],"keyword":["COVID","Collaboration across distance","Comparative studies","Distance","Ethnography","Global Software Development","Hybrid work","Incongruence","Synchronous interaction","Workshop"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"achieving symmetry in synchronous interaction in hybrid work is impossible","src":["au","ku","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3648617","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3648617","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-097.66-767390","identifier":[{"id":"85194869938","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-3f48fe57-3c50-4e6c-afc8-c205a2958be8","ku-87a42baf-7f64-4907-aaee-490cea50593e","ruc-f349c6c8-0592-4a52-b82f-b3a364be57ac"],"keyword":["Covid","Distance","collaboration across distance","comparative studies","ethnography","global software development","hybrid work","incongruence","synchronous interaction","workshop"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-63DE91D29947C8BD99378BAF85062FF6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/397906579/Achieving_Symmetry.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3648617","type":"loc","url":"https://rucforsk.ruc.dk/ws/files/107075131/3648617.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3648617","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3648617","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology","Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology, Sustainable Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Pernille","key":"bjørnpau","last":"Bjørn","locid":"8578cc6c-33b6-46fb-b85a-36299bd08e91","name":"Bjørn, Pernille","orcid":"0000-0001-7049-1173","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","first":"Juliane","key":"busboompau","last":"Busboom","locid":"e257abd9-f9c8-4497-9358-b8a9f7f28d2f","name":"Busboom, Juliane Brigitta","name_org":"Busboom, Juliane","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Melanie","key":"duckertpau","last":"Duckert","name":"Duckert, Melanie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Susanne","key":"bødkerpau","last":"Bødker","locid":"87d4fbb6-b38c-449e-b87d-59f693b7d6f0","name":"Bødker, Susanne","orcid":"0000-0002-3571-1617","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Irina","key":"shklovskipau","last":"Shklovski","locid":"e5067505-4088-4c76-b398-aedd455ed164","name":"Shklovski, Irina","orcid":"0000-0003-1874-0958","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eve","key":"hogganpau","last":"Hoggan","locid":"2eb4e432-9116-4a75-8144-3766c07762bc","name":"Hoggan, Eve","orcid":"0000-0002-2563-4320","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kellie","key":"dunnpau","last":"Dunn","locid":"6cf75e5c-0592-43bd-b794-be7d53c76915","name":"Dunn, Kellie","orcid":"0000-0002-2731-9789","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Qianqian","key":"mupau","last":"Mu","locid":"7eabae73-9121-4cc3-a605-d900be976e08","name":"Mu, Qianqian","orcid":"0000-0002-5114-806X","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"barkhuuspau","last":"Barkhuus","name":"Barkhuus, Louise","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","first":"Nina","key":"boulusrødjepau","last":"Boulus-Rødje","locid":"eb7b15a4-1bca-48da-9fe7-039040ba3f4d","name":"Boulus-Rødje, Nina","orcid":"0000-0002-0837-0853","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3648617","issn":["10730516","15577325"],"issn_org":["10730516"],"issue":"4","journal":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","paperid":"49","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","volume":"31"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85194869938"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-3f48fe57-3c50-4e6c-afc8-c205a2958be8","ku-87a42baf-7f64-4907-aaee-490cea50593e","ruc-f349c6c8-0592-4a52-b82f-b3a364be57ac"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Achieving Symmetry in Synchronous Interaction in Hybrid Work is Impossible","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-097.66-767390","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Being part of a minority group can be isolating and lonely; in some places, members of LGBTQ+ groups build up communities and neighborhoods, but in other areas, LGBTQ+ places are more invisible. In this paper we present a system that facilitates a digital layer of stories and experiences by other LGBTQ+ individuals. We present our novel prototype and our user study of the application, where users discuss how this digital service can help building place based community among LGBTQ+. This study contributes by providing a better understanding of how the physical space of the city can be transformed into meaningful places through a digital layer of personal stories to encourage connections within the LGBTQ+ population.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Neupart, Mack","Ashbrook, Daniel","Barkhuus, Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-809658"],"event":{"end":"2024-05-16","place":"Hybrid, Honolulu, us","start":"2024-05-11","title":"2024 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Sytems, CHI EA 2024","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["LGBTQ","Location based services","Mobile Applications","Place"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"queering the space location based stories for transforming space into place","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-809658","identifier":[{"id":"85194186490","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-4863b94b-8796-4c6c-9bdc-ab3151be5560"],"keyword":["Interactive Experiences","LGBTQ+","Location-Based Services","Mobile Applications","Place"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CF6836CFE723AA2D824A860FBF446B58"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Mack","key":"neupartpau","last":"Neupart","name":"Neupart, Mack","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Daniel","key":"ashbrookpau","last":"Ashbrook","locid":"bb940bf4-14b7-4f3b-bedf-154170369515","name":"Ashbrook, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0002-4326-2110","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Louise","key":"barkhuuspau","last":"Barkhuus","name":"Barkhuus, Louise","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Florian Floyd","key":"muellerpedh","last":"Mueller","name":"Mueller, Florian Floyd","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Penny","key":"kyburzpedh","last":"Kyburz","name":"Kyburz, Penny","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Julie R.","key":"williamsonpedh","last":"Williamson","name":"Williamson, Julie R.","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Corina","key":"saspedh","last":"Sas","name":"Sas, Corina","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3613905.3650885","eisbn":["9798400703317"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI 2024 - Extended Abstracts of the 2024 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Sytems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85194186490"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4863b94b-8796-4c6c-9bdc-ab3151be5560"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Queering the Space : Location-Based Stories for Transforming Space into Place","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-809658","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Abundant research has investigated gender differ- ences from the perspective of the individual, but little research has explored gender differences from a group perspective. This paper presents a survey-based quantitative exploration of gender differences when N=138 students working in smaller (3 stu- dents) groups that are either men-majority (WMM) or men- only (MMM). The study was conducted during four weeks of CS1 project work, and the investigation focused on student struggle, task division, group satisfaction, and course outcomes. We observed four significant gender differences: (i) Many women students express a lack of confidence early in the project work, as do many men students that are part of mixed-gender (WMM) groups, but not men that are part of men-only (MMM) groups; (ii) In men-majority groups, women perform more ‘non- technical’ tasks than men (e.g., analysis, design, and report editing); and (iii) women students in men-majority groups are less satisfied with several aspects of the group work than men students in the same groups. These findings add to our understanding of group dynamics and collaboration (including what to avoid and where to pay extra attention) when assigning projects in smaller groups in computing education.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Westh, Bjørn Hjorth","Inie, Nanna","Barkhuus, Louise","Brabrand, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718933"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computing education","Confidence in project work","Gender differences","Group dynamics","Group satisfaction","Men majority groups","Mixed gender groups","Non technical tasks","Project work outcomes","Task division"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"gender differences in the group dynamics of smaller cs000000000001 project groups","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102753129/FIE-2023-struggle_pdf.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This project is partially funded by DIREC P31 with the intention of uncovering knowledge important for the recruitment and retention of students in Computer Science. One recurring discussion is how to form student groups and whether to let students form groups autonomously or to have course instructors form groups (and on which basis). This study was part of an initiative to understand how these groups work in practice, in order to be able to make evidence-based recommendations and decisions about this in the future."],"id":"S01-000.00-718933","ids":["itu-f48d14a6-4f0b-4ef6-85f9-64b989c0ebb1"],"keyword":["Computing education","Confidence in project work","Gender differences","Group dynamics","Group satisfaction","Men-majority groups","Mixed-gender groups","Non-technical tasks","Project work outcomes","Task division"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-61C0194325C7E4E2D053065FF7A5E20D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102753129/FIE-2023-struggle_pdf.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://ccer.itu.dk/-/media/86E7BD9D7CD147E3AE014795C9AE42D3.ashx","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Bjørn Hjorth","key":"westhpau","last":"Westh","name":"Westh, Bjørn Hjorth","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"barkhuuspau","last":"Barkhuus","locid":"831421c0-027f-4a25-b795-ee2fce0c8d2a","name":"Barkhuus, Louise","orcid":"0000-0003-1306-249X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","locid":"47a94200-3abe-4354-b5e3-c8ef6f1ba1f2","name":"Brabrand, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-5093-5806","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"place":"College Station, Texas, USA","status":"p","title":"IEEE ASEE Frontiers in Education Conference 2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f48d14a6-4f0b-4ef6-85f9-64b989c0ebb1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Gender Differences in the Group Dynamics of Smaller CS1 Project Groups","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718933","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"After the pandemic, it is urgently important to explore the special challenges which arise with hybrid work. Through cross-case analyses of published papers, we propose collocated distance as a design challenge uniquely relevant for hybrid cooperative technologies. We identify and conceptualize collocated distance as a design challenge that arises in hybrid work situations, where at least three actors are mutually dependent in their work while being located within fewer contexts than the number of actors. Collocated distance reminds us that when designing hybrid technologies, we must not only focus on creating technologies that support the work across geographical locations but equally pay attention to the relations and possible disconnections which exist locally between collocated actors. When designing cooperative technologies supporting distributed work, often focus is on the boundaries between geographical contexts - however, in hybrid work, we must not forget to pay attention to the collocated boundaries within the same context.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Duckert, Melanie","Barkhuus, Louise","Bjørn, Pernille"],"cuid":["S02-089.78-725103"],"event":{"end":"2023-04-28","place":"Hamburg, de","start":"2023-04-23","title":"2023 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI 2023","year":"2023"},"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Cooperative work","Distributed work","Hybrid work"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"collocated distance a fundamental challenge for the design of hybrid work technologies","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/411066804/Collocated_Distance.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-089.78-725103","identifier":[{"id":"85160022078","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b9104821-7633-45fd-96a4-6c9aead11a03","ku-2dba9af9-9fc2-4d2b-bb22-e5088eb21f00"],"keyword":["cooperative work","distributed work","hybrid work"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-CB38827DCDB0DEE46694D4D4C8FE501A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/103391880/chi23-218.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/411066804/Collocated_Distance.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Melanie","key":"duckertpau","last":"Duckert","locid":"a0f5a47c-1073-4c01-b62d-51b767f01d69","name":"Duckert, Melanie","orcid":"0000-0001-8079-8363","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"barkhuuspau","last":"Barkhuus","locid":"831421c0-027f-4a25-b795-ee2fce0c8d2a","name":"Barkhuus, Louise","orcid":"0000-0003-1306-249X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Pernille","key":"bjørnpau","last":"Bjørn","locid":"8578cc6c-33b6-46fb-b85a-36299bd08e91","name":"Bjørn, Pernille","orcid":"0000-0001-7049-1173","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3544548.3580899","eisbn":["9781450394215"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery, Inc.","status":"p","title":"CHI 2023 - Proceedings of the 2023 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160022078"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b9104821-7633-45fd-96a4-6c9aead11a03","ku-2dba9af9-9fc2-4d2b-bb22-e5088eb21f00"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Collocated Distance : A Fundamental Challenge for the Design of Hybrid Work Technologies","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-089.78-725103","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Remote and hybrid work has received significant attention in the last years in both academic and industrial contexts, especially due to the COVID-19 pandemic and attendant lockdown. Some of the remaining challenges in current remote technologies include limited embodiment, hierarchy and agency issues, and significant technological limitations. In this workshop, we will, together with the participants, explore how to design socio-technical systems that connect people and artefacts during collaborative activities. The workshop will use provocations, artefacts, and group work to imagine the futures of work with a focus on hybrid work practices.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Duckert, Melanie","Hoggan, Eve","Barkhuus, Louise","Bjørn, Pernille","Boulus-Rødje, Nina","Bødker, Susanne","Holten Møller, Naja","Shlovski, Irina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-558871"],"event":{"end":"2022-10-12","place":"Aarhus","start":"2022-10-10","title":"Nordic Human-Computer Interaction Conference 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["CSCW","Embodiment","Hybrid work","Materiality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"work of the future","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-558871","identifier":[{"id":"85139861522","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-2b66e68b-22be-4add-9020-b345a8bf3612"],"keyword":["CSCW","embodiment","future ofr work","hybrid work","materiality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-6-C0E286647D3211E201918A60B6B37491"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities"